WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.002
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book one.

2
00:00:05.302 --> 00:00:08.711
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.011 --> 00:00:15.634
Unit nineteen: Legacy Building. Today you will describe lasting effects with beyond + noun phrase.

4
00:00:15.934 --> 00:00:18.691
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:22.901
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:24.705
Legacy.

7
00:00:25.717 --> 00:00:29.260
Legacy means what someone leaves behind that continues to matter.

8
00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:32.697
Her legacy is a network of community clinics.

9
00:00:33.283 --> 00:00:35.344
Your turn. Say: Legacy.

10
00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:39.422
Legacy.

11
00:00:40.183 --> 00:00:41.614
Long-term impact.

12
00:00:42.617 --> 00:00:46.427
Long-term impact means an effect that continues for many years.

13
00:00:47.017 --> 00:00:51.315
The scholarship's long-term impact is visible across the province.

14
00:00:51.917 --> 00:00:54.708
Your turn. Say: Long-term impact.

15
00:00:57.567 --> 00:01:00.843
Long-term impact.

16
00:01:01.583 --> 00:01:02.910
Succession planning.

17
00:01:03.917 --> 00:01:08.018
Succession planning means preparing others to take over leadership roles.

18
00:01:08.617 --> 00:01:11.951
Succession planning ensured the school survived its founder.

19
00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:15.330
Your turn. Say: Succession planning.

20
00:01:18.167 --> 00:01:19.679
Succession planning.

21
00:01:20.417 --> 00:01:22.103
Institutional memory.

22
00:01:23.117 --> 00:01:27.798
Institutional memory means knowledge and experience retained by an organisation.

23
00:01:28.383 --> 00:01:32.031
Documentation preserves institutional memory when staff leave.

24
00:01:32.617 --> 00:01:35.745
Your turn. Say: Institutional memory.

25
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.530
Institutional memory.

26
00:01:41.283 --> 00:01:42.331
Mentorship.

27
00:01:43.317 --> 00:01:47.522
Mentorship means guidance from an experienced person to a less experienced one.

28
00:01:48.117 --> 00:01:51.045
Mentorship is the most personal form of legacy.

29
00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:54.465
Your turn. Say: Mentorship.

30
00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:58.301
Mentorship.

31
00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:00.365
Sustainability.

32
00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:04.406
Sustainability means the ability to continue over time.

33
00:02:05.017 --> 00:02:07.887
Local funding secured the project's sustainability.

34
00:02:08.483 --> 00:02:11.565
Your turn. Say: Sustainability.

35
00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:15.842
Sustainability.

36
00:02:16.583 --> 00:02:17.596
Values.

37
00:02:18.583 --> 00:02:21.964
Values means principles that guide behaviour and decisions.

38
00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:25.467
The founder's values still guide hiring decisions.

39
00:02:26.083 --> 00:02:28.979
Your turn. Say: Values.

40
00:02:31.833 --> 00:02:32.881
Values.

41
00:02:33.617 --> 00:02:34.711
Contribution.

42
00:02:35.717 --> 00:02:39.330
Contribution means something given to help a cause or community.

43
00:02:39.917 --> 00:02:42.857
His contribution to Khmer literature is enormous.

44
00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:46.509
Your turn. Say: Contribution.

45
00:02:49.367 --> 00:02:50.472
Contribution.

46
00:02:51.267 --> 00:02:54.720
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:55.583 --> 00:03:00.836
Visal, the founder of a famous Siem Reap school has just died. Will the school survive?

48
00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:11.104
I believe so. Her influence extends far beyond her own lifetime, because she trained former students to become its leaders.

49
00:03:12.217 --> 00:03:15.168
Isn't the school really built around her personality?

50
00:03:16.283 --> 00:03:25.635
It was at first. But she deliberately built systems, values and institutional memory that reach beyond any single person.

51
00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:28.993
So legacy is mainly about planning?

52
00:03:30.083 --> 00:03:39.202
Planning and generosity. A true legacy lasts beyond any single career, because others have been empowered to continue and improve the work.

53
00:03:40.367 --> 00:03:42.320
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:43.183 --> 00:03:46.820
Grammar focus: Beyond plus noun phrase (lasting effect).

55
00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:48.400
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:49.083 --> 00:03:52.418
A true legacy lasts beyond any single career.

57
00:03:53.317 --> 00:03:56.268
Her influence extends far beyond her own lifetime.

58
00:03:57.183 --> 00:03:59.938
Good systems reach beyond any single person.

59
00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:11.412
'Beyond' is a preposition followed directly by a noun phrase (beyond a career — not 'beyond of'). Intensify with 'far', 'well' or 'long': far beyond, well beyond, long beyond.

60
00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:16.254
Be careful! Don't say: A legacy lasts beyond of a career.

61
00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:21.012
Say: A legacy lasts beyond a career.

62
00:04:21.312 --> 00:04:22.940
Beyond + noun (no 'of').

63
00:04:23.733 --> 00:04:26.362
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:27.167 --> 00:04:28.922
Building Beyond Ourselves.

65
00:04:29.222 --> 00:04:36.110
Many leaders aspire to leave a legacy, yet relatively few organisations outlive their founders by more than a generation.

66
00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:44.452
Research on family businesses and NGOs suggests that the difference lies in succession planning and the transfer of institutional memory.

67
00:04:45.467 --> 00:04:52.331
Leaders who centralise every decision may achieve remarkable results, but their influence rarely extends beyond their own tenure.

68
00:04:53.333 --> 00:04:59.942
By contrast, leaders who invest in mentorship and clear values create organisations capable of renewing themselves.

69
00:05:00.933 --> 00:05:06.729
Legacy, therefore, is less about personal recognition than about building something that continues to grow beyond oneself.

70
00:05:07.833 --> 00:05:11.812
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:15.195
His fame spread far blank Cambodia.

72
00:05:15.495 --> 00:05:19.253
A, beyond. B, beyond of. C, beyond to.

73
00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:24.881
The answer is A: beyond.

74
00:05:26.017 --> 00:05:28.713
Knowledge retained by an organisation is blank.

75
00:05:29.013 --> 00:05:33.792
A, institutional memory. B, values. C, sustainability.

76
00:05:37.333 --> 00:05:40.447
The answer is A: institutional memory.

77
00:05:41.583 --> 00:05:44.663
Preparing others to take over leadership roles is blank.

78
00:05:44.963 --> 00:05:49.510
A, contribution. B, mentorship. C, succession planning.

79
00:05:53.067 --> 00:05:55.635
The answer is C: succession planning.

80
00:05:56.783 --> 00:05:58.667
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:58.967 --> 00:06:07.682
A, Her work lasted well beyond her retirement.. B, Her work lasted well beyond of her retirement.. C, Her work lasted beyond to her retirement..

82
00:06:11.233 --> 00:06:15.149
The answer is A: Her work lasted well beyond her retirement..

83
00:06:16.283 --> 00:06:19.699
According to the reading, leaders who centralise every decision…

84
00:06:19.999 --> 00:06:26.671
A, build lasting organisations. B, rarely extend influence beyond their tenure. C, always succeed.

85
00:06:30.233 --> 00:06:34.149
The answer is B: rarely extend influence beyond their tenure.

86
00:06:35.333 --> 00:06:38.705
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:43.384
What kind of legacy would you like to leave in your community or career?

88
00:07:00.183 --> 00:07:01.695
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:01.995 --> 00:07:37.904
In my community, I would like to leave a legacy of educational opportunity that extends beyond my own lifetime. Ideally, I would help establish a small learning centre where rural children could access books, technology and mentors. More importantly, I would train young volunteers to manage it, so that the project continues long after I step back. A legacy that depends on one person is fragile, whereas one built on shared values and capable successors can grow for generations.

90
00:07:38.204 --> 00:07:39.855
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:45.887
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and beyond plus noun phrase (lasting effect).

92
00:07:46.187 --> 00:07:50.987
Today's value is legacy. Live in a way that leaves the world better for those who follow.

93
00:07:51.287 --> 00:07:53.974
See you in Unit twenty. Goodbye!

94
00:07:54.274 --> 00:07:56.961
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

